# Active engine plan

- [ ] **Stage 5 — proactive context compaction.**
  - Current branch: work; inspected head before Stage 5 summary runner: d0404afcf1a4d05a8b5b2c5d901820eb4a9454c5.
  - Stage 4 is complete and CI-verified: exact OpenAI API input-token preflight; Gemini Developer API approximate countTokens preflight; confidence-aware policy (approximate estimates never hard-block); verified model capability/context metadata; output reserve and safety headroom; shared accounting; one safe compaction/recount attempt; native tool-call pairing and canonical history preservation. Do not redo Stage 4.
  - **Completed step 1 — architecture inspection:** compaction is synchronous at the RunContext turn boundary. maybeCompactContext checks previous provider usage, and compactActiveContext copies prefix/tail, makes one summary Generate call, persists a context_snapshot, then replaces a.messages. Errors before snapshot persistence retain active context and canonical records. contextCompactionCut validates complete call/result boundaries and retains at least six tail messages; snapshots preserve NativeSteps. resumeEntry restores the latest snapshot plus canonical records after it. Session Message metadata currently carries only ContextSnapshotCount—no source boundary/version token.
    - Ownership: a.messages is mutated by RunContext, Clear, Rewind, NewSession and resumeEntry; there is no dedicated messages mutex or Agent run mutex. Normal TUI operation serializes prompts with working/submitOnEnter and only Ctrl-C can interrupt; HTTP generation routes share chatMu and wait for runDone after cancellation; scheduler/team workers use separate Agent instances. These frontend guards do not make Agent itself concurrency-safe.
    - Reads/callbacks: /context reads ContextStatus and ContextChars; ContextChars iterates a.messages without a lock. ChatMessages uses activityMu, but direct RunContext appends to chatMessages and some direct history.Add calls do not use that lock. TUI text/event sinks forward to buffered UI channels; server sinks forward to an event channel. They do not themselves mutate a.messages. Server plain GET can read ChatMessages while RunContext is running, so its current activityMu protection is incomplete.
    - Persistence/cancellation: Session.Store.Add has no internal mutex; callers currently serialize most writes. RunContext and synchronous summary share the run context, so cancellation/errors normally prevent snapshot application. Server cancellation waits for runDone; TUI cancellation waits for the done event. A future worker must not touch the live message slice or Store from its goroutine.
  - Completed step 2: added a deep-copied `contextCompactionCandidate` with session path, source revision, reset epoch, safe cut and bounded summary input. Its pure rebase validator accepts append-only growth while retaining newly appended messages and NativeSteps; it rejects a changed session/epoch, regressed revision, replaced/shortened source prefix, untracked append, incomplete tool-call tail, invalid cut and empty summary. Unit tests cover source aliasing, tool-pair/native-step retention and stale-state cases. CI run [#44443793950](https://github.com/AmrUser-48/agy-open/actions/runs/44443793950) passed tests, vet and Linux build.
  - Completed step 3: `activeContextVersion()` and `noteActiveContextMutation()` track monotonic message revision and reset epoch. All direct `a.messages` append/replacement/truncation sites in RunContext, resume, Clear, Rewind and compaction are instrumented. Tests cover normal turn appends, destructive lifecycle changes, resume reconstruction and compaction. This metadata does not lock or make the live slice concurrency-safe. CI run [#44444468099](https://github.com/AmrUser-48/agy-open/actions/runs/44444468099) passed tests, vet and Linux build.
  - Completed step 4: `activeContextMu` now guards the live message slice, revision and reset epoch. All production reads/writes in RunContext, resume, clear, rewind, request assembly, ContextChars/ContextStatus and compaction use locked accessors. Compaction captures a stable slice/version and validates that revision/epoch still match while holding the lock before it persists a snapshot and replaces context; an append/reset during summary safely discards the stale result without writing a snapshot. Concurrent append/snapshot tests and stale-summary invalidation test pass. CI run [#44445258566](https://github.com/AmrUser-48/agy-open/actions/runs/44445258566) passed tests, vet and Linux build.
  - Completed step 5: added pure `PlanProactiveCompaction`. Exact counts use 80% early-preparation and 95% safety-barrier targets; approximate counts use conservative 75%/90% targets. It consumes the already-adjusted usable input budget (fixed request overhead included by the counter, output reserve and safety headroom removed) and returns no-op for unknown/invalid budgets. Tests cover threshold boundaries, over-budget statuses, confidence handling, and integer-ceiling overflow safety. CI run [#44445710800](https://github.com/AmrUser-48/agy-open/actions/runs/44445710800) passed tests, vet and Linux build.
  - Completed step 6 — worker design findings: schedule only after provider usage and model content are recorded/appended, before executing local tools, so summary generation can overlap tool work. At a later RunContext boundary, poll/apply a completed result before appending the next user prompt; use immutable candidate + revision/epoch + append-only rebase + `commitActiveContextReplacement`. Start with `openai-api` and `gemini-api` only: both are API-key HTTP clients and their Generate methods have no whole-request serialization lock (OpenAI's mutable reasoning cache is `sync.Map`; Gemini Developer API path uses the shared concurrency-safe HTTP client and does not enter the OAuth project mutex). AI Studio/Interactions locks the whole Generate call and would serialize the next generation; exclude it initially. Gemini Live creates a separate WebSocket session and has no verified usable context-window metadata; exclude it. Antigravity/OpenAI OAuth and generic/OpenRouter/Groq/NVIDIA stay excluded until equivalent window/counter and concurrency guarantees are established.
    - One cancellable worker per Agent, protected by a dedicated mutex. Worker uses a detached `context.WithTimeout(context.Background(), 90*time.Second)` plus an explicit cancel; successful RunContext completion must not cancel it (TUI cancels its request context after success), but a RunContext that exits because its parent context was actually canceled must cancel it. `Clear`, `NewSession`, resume, `Rewind`, and `Close` cancel and wait before resetting/closing session state. A completed result remains in memory until the next request boundary or lifecycle cancellation.
    - Goroutine may read only the immutable deep-copied candidate and call `model.Generate`; it must not read/write `a.messages`, append to `Session.Store`, call `recordProviderUsage`/`recordContextUsage`, or touch the current `RequestAccounting`. Existing `recordModelAdapterCall`/`recordRequestUsage` mutate the one active prompt's counters, so background completion must carry only summary text, Usage, and error back to the owner goroutine. Attribute/persist it only when the result is validated and applied at a request boundary, with separate proactive-compaction accounting to avoid charging the previous/next prompt incorrectly.
    - A failed/canceled/stale worker is optional preparation failure: discard its result, keep current active/canonical history unchanged, set a cooldown to avoid immediate retry storms, and continue to the existing synchronous Stage 4 safety gate. At ~95% safety barrier, wait only for a bounded interval for the existing worker; if it is not ready, use the current synchronous gate/compaction rather than waiting indefinitely. Never spawn a second worker while one is pending or completed-but-unapplied.
  - Completed step 7: added one-worker-per-Agent state, detached 90-second context, bounded/non-blocking result handoff, panic-to-error conversion, cancellation/wait/disposal, and permanent close guard. Clear, NewSession, resume, Rewind and Close cancel/wait; a RunContext exiting because its own context is canceled also cancels, while successful completion leaves the detached worker alive. Tests cover single-worker enforcement, success, timeout, panic, bounded polling, clear/restart, rewind, resume, new session, close and canceled RunContext. CI run [#44447973920](https://github.com/AmrUser-48/agy-open/actions/runs/44447973920) passed tests, vet and Linux build.
  - **Active step 8:** implement the provider-limited proactive summary runner, but do not schedule it. Allow only `openai-api` and `gemini-api`. Build a compact summary request from the immutable candidate, call `model.Generate` with the worker context, and return summary text plus provider usage/error only. Do not mutate Agent messages, session history, usage totals, RequestAccounting, event sinks or diagnostics from the worker. Tests must prove unsupported providers are rejected, request includes the candidate prefix and configured summary instructions, cancellation propagates, and foreground counters/history remain unchanged.
  - **Policy targets:** approximately 80% for early preparation and 95% for a safety barrier, adjusted for fixed request overhead, output reserve, estimator confidence and provider limits. These are policy targets, not hard-coded universal limits.
  - **Required invariants:** summarize only structurally complete prefixes; retain the untouched tail and all newly appended messages; canonical archive remains immutable/resumable; one summary request by default; no summary failure may abort an otherwise valid turn; no dangling artifact references.
  - **Tests:** valid cut boundaries; stale snapshot rejection/rebase; concurrent appends; cancellation/failure fallback; cooldown; snapshot resume; canonical archive equality; artifact-reference integrity.
